1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,600
The final section of this chapter deals with the clinical characteristics of neoplasia.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,500
And the idea here is that once you go through the sequence of carcinogenesis and tumor progression,

3
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:16,960
eventually the patients will present with certain important clinical characteristics,

4
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,260
and so that's what we want to discuss here.

5
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:23,880
Now, we're not going to discuss specific characteristics for each individual tumor,

6
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:28,940
but we're going to discuss some general concepts that you need to have concerning neoplasia

7
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,540
and how it might present clinically.

8
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:36,300
So obviously, we know that neoplasia can be divided into benign and malignant.

9
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:39,280
So there are benign tumors and there are malignant tumors.

10
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:43,660
Generally speaking, benign tumors are going to be slow-growing.

11
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:51,380
The patient may give a history of having a mass that has grown slowly over many years, for example.

12
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:55,100
Another characteristic is that the tumor will be well-circumscribed,

13
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,560
and that would be where you would examine the tumor clinically,

14
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:04,680
and it's clearly distinct and separate from the adjacent tissue in which it's growing.

15
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:07,220
So, for example, a patient has a breast mass,

16
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:11,680
and the breast mass is clearly distinct and separate from the remainder of the breast tissue.

17
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,160
And so that's basically what we mean by distinct here.

18
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:15,640
And finally, it's mobile.

19
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:17,020
And what does mobile mean?

20
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,900
It means that if you take the mass and try to move it around,

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,200
for example, if it was a breast mass, and you try to move it around,

22
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,460
it's easy to move the mass around.

23
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:26,900
And what does that tell you?

24
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,280
That tells you that the mass is not invading into local tissues,

25
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:34,660
it's not attaching itself, for example, to the chest wall in the case of the breast.

26
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:40,200
And so these are some classic clinical features that we would expect in benign tumors.

27
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:45,040
In contrast, malignant tumors would have the following characteristics.

28
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:47,720
Malignant tumors are usually rapidly growing.

29
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:54,340
The patient usually gives a history of a tumor that's grown within a year or grown sometimes within months.

30
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:55,720
They're poorly circumscribed.

31
00:01:56,520 --> 00:02:00,740
You try to sort of get your hand around the tumor, for example, if it's a breast cancer,

32
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:07,120
and you can't really separate it from the adjacent surrounding normal breast tissue.

33
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,480
It infiltrates into local tissues, and it's fixed to surrounding structures.

34
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:17,500
It's not mobile, but instead it's attached, for example, to the chest wall in the case of breast cancer.

35
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:23,700
And so these are some classic clinical features concerning benign and malignant tumors.

36
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:30,420
It should be noted, however, that sometimes a benign tumor can have malignant characteristics,

37
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,100
and sometimes a malignant tumor can have benign characteristics.

38
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:37,740
And so these clinical features are simply screening tests.

39
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:43,040
The only way to make an absolute diagnosis of cancer is biopsy or excision.

40
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:49,240
Otherwise stated, it's the pathologist who makes the final determination of cancer.

41
00:02:49,760 --> 00:03:00,440
The only way, again, to make an absolute certain diagnosis of cancer is that the tissue must be biopsied or it must be excised.

42
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:07,920
And so that's an important principle to bear in mind, and that will come into play later when we have some discussions about screening tests as well.

43
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:10,980
Now, what are some of the histologic features of cancer?

44
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:19,300
If cancer is going to be determined on biopsy by looking at the cells under the microscope and clearly calling them malignant,

45
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:23,100
then we need to understand the histologic features of cancer as well.

46
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:30,640
Now, generally, when we think about benign tumors, they look very similar to the tissue in which they're growing.

47
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,320
For example, the benign tumors usually have an organized growth.

48
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,160
The nuclei will be uniform.

49
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:41,840
The nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio will be low.

50
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:42,680
What does that mean?

51
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:47,020
It means there will be a lot of cytoplasm in the cell and maybe a small nucleus.

52
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,340
That gives you a low nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio.

53
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:57,420
There will be minimal mitotic activity, a lack of invasion, and this tumor will have no metastatic potential,

54
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:06,800
meaning that the tumor will not have the ability to spread outside of that particular organ in that patient or in any other patient.

55
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:07,620
All right?

56
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:11,900
And so that's how we understand no metastatic potential.

57
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:15,660
And, again, this word potential is important because what we do is when we see,

58
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,800
when a biopsy is performed and I see a certain pattern and I call that, let's say, for example,

59
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:24,120
I call that a benign tumor, like in the breast, I call that a fibroadenoma,

60
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:31,200
I know that fibroadenomas don't metastasize historically in all the patients that have had fibroadenoma.

61
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:33,720
And that's what makes it benign.

62
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:38,980
And so it has no metastatic potential in the patient who's now developed a fibroadenoma as well.

63
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:42,500
So those are some histologic features of benign tumors.

64
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:46,540
The histologic features of malignant tumors are as follows.

65
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:51,920
It's usually poorly differentiated, and I say usually because it doesn't always have to be.

66
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,840
It'll have disorganized growth where it doesn't really look like the tissue in which it's growing.

67
00:04:58,360 --> 00:04:59,500
There'll be nuclear pleomorphism.

68
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:03,840
Some of the nuclei will be big and some of the nuclei will be small.

69
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:05,500
There'll be hyperchromasia.

70
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,720
The nuclei will be very dark blue.

71
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:10,600
There'll be a high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio.

72
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:15,060
So if this is the cell, most of the cell will be comprised of nucleus.

73
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:17,480
And what does that indicate?

74
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,540
That indicates that the cell is very active and that the nucleus is completely open

75
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:25,360
and is rapidly transcribing genes, etc.

76
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:31,320
There'll be a high mitotic activity, indicating that the cells are rapidly dividing.

77
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:34,820
And there'll be invasion into local structures.

78
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:41,320
And so these are some of the histologic hallmarks that help us to classify a tumor as benign or malignant.

79
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:49,300
Now, here's an example that I've placed in your text to highlight some of these principles.

80
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:51,640
And we're basically comparing and contrasting.

81
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:58,680
So this picture on the left, this is an example of a follicular adenoma of the thyroid.

82
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:02,220
Now, a follicular adenoma of the thyroid is a benign tumor.

83
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:08,920
And when you look at the tumor, it shows many of the characteristics that we would expect to see in a benign tumor.

84
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:16,200
For example, here we can clearly see that these cells, by the way, the entire picture here,

85
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,140
this entire picture that I'm squaring away represents the tumor.

86
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:24,440
So here we can clearly see that the cells are organizing themselves in little round spaces,

87
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:26,700
as you would expect to see within the thyroid.

88
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:31,680
Remember that the thyroid makes follicles, and the follicles are lined by follicular cells.

89
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,300
And often in the center of the follicle, you see colloid.

90
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:40,060
And you can clearly see some pink colloid in the center of these round nests of cells.

91
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:43,820
In addition, you can see that the nuclei are sort of regular.

92
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:47,400
All the cells sort of have round, similar-looking nuclei.

93
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,940
You don't really see any mitotic activity.

94
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,500
The cells don't have a very high N-to-C ratio.

95
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:57,320
In fact, when you look at the nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio,

96
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:02,020
you can clearly see that if this is a cell here, the nucleus is small,

97
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,380
and there's a lot of cytoplasm above it.

98
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,780
So these are all features that we would expect to see in a benign tumor.

99
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:13,460
And indeed, on the left, we have this benign thyroid tumor, i.e. a follicular adenoma.

100
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:19,560
Now, on the right, what I've exhibited here is something called anaplastic carcinoma of the thyroid.

101
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:27,020
Now, when you look at this tumor, you can tell that clearly it's not making any nice round spaces with colloid in the middle.

102
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:31,180
You can see that the cells are larger than the cells on the left.

103
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:33,440
You can see that there's mitotic activity.

104
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,100
This is a mitotic figure here.

105
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:41,020
You can see that the nuclei are big, and you can see that they take up most of the cell.

106
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:46,260
And so all of these are features of histologic features of a malignant tumor.

107
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:52,380
Now, it's important to note, however, that, again, benign tumors can sometimes have malignant features,

108
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:55,540
and malignant tumors can sometimes have benign features.

109
00:07:55,860 --> 00:08:00,540
So what is the absolute distinguishing feature between benign and malignant?

110
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:02,480
And this is what examiners like you to know.

111
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:07,740
The absolute distinguishing feature between benign and malignant is metastatic potential.

112
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,340
Benign tumors do not have metastatic potential.

113
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:14,680
Malignant tumors have metastatic potential.

114
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:21,660
So this is a very important take-home point because it's the absolute distinction between benign and malignant tumors.

115
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:30,520
One important clinically useful test in this context of neoplasia is immunohistochemistry.

116
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:37,080
And the way we use immunohistochemistry depends on the circumstance that we're challenged with.

117
00:08:37,340 --> 00:08:39,960
But let me just give you a couple simple examples.

118
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:45,340
Let's pretend that I have a very poorly differentiated cancer.

119
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:47,200
What does poorly differentiated mean?

120
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,100
It looks nothing like any normal tissue.

121
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:51,880
So the patient has a mass.

122
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:53,440
They do a biopsy.

123
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:58,600
And when I get the biopsy, I see that the cells are poorly differentiated.

124
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:01,440
They don't look like anything that we're used to seeing.

125
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:05,900
And so when I see those cells, in the back of my mind, I begin to think,

126
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:07,840
huh, is this carcinoma?

127
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:09,540
Meaning, is it epithelial?

128
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:12,720
Well, is it lymphoma, i.e., are the cells lymphocytes?

129
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,140
Is it sarcoma, i.e., are the cells derived from mesenchyme?

130
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,040
Are they some sort of connective tissue cell?

131
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:21,040
Or is it melanoma, for example?

132
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:27,860
And I start with a very broad differential diagnosis because these are all my common subtypes of cancer.

133
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:34,560
Now, I can look at the cells and try to use morphology to help me to determine what cell type I'm dealing with.

134
00:09:34,940 --> 00:09:40,660
But another useful technique to determine the type of cell is called immunohistochemistry.

135
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:46,040
The idea here is that we take an antibody against a particular target,

136
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:51,960
and we label that antibody with a brown stain, and then we place that antibody on the cells.

137
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:57,960
And if the antibody binds the cells, then it helps us to identify particular proteins

138
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:01,280
that might help us to subclassify the cell type.

139
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:07,160
And it's important to be familiar with this because, of course, it's useful clinically.

140
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:13,800
And also examiners like to go after it because it really draws in some of the basic principles of histology.

141
00:10:14,020 --> 00:10:16,420
So this is a great way to sort of test your histology.

142
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:20,320
Now, let me remind you that cells have a cytoskeleton,

143
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:27,140
and the cytoskeleton contains filaments, and one of those filaments is the intermediate filaments.

144
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:30,800
One subtype of filament of the cytoskeleton is the intermediate filament.

145
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:36,600
Now, it so happens that the intermediate filaments, they vary depending on the type of cell that you're dealing with.

146
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:39,840
So, for example, if we're dealing with an epithelial cell,

147
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:47,240
and we can say that all epithelial cells, the intermediate filament that makes them up is keratin.

148
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:53,600
So let's say that I have a tumor, and I can't recognize whether the tumor cells are epithelial or something else,

149
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:56,120
and I wanted to prove that they're epithelial.

150
00:10:56,580 --> 00:11:00,100
Well, I could do an immunohistochemical stain for keratin,

151
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:06,520
and if the cells are keratin positive, that would indicate that I'm dealing with a carcinoma.

152
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:11,560
Because, remember, carcinoma is the name that we use for malignant epithelial tumors.

153
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:13,500
All right, let's play it another way.

154
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,360
Let's pretend that I've got this tumor, and I do an immunohistochemical stain,

155
00:11:17,660 --> 00:11:20,300
and I see that the cells are positive for vimentin.

156
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:26,580
Well, vimentin is an intermediate filament that is present within mesenchymal cells, i.e., connective tissue cells.

157
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:35,560
And so if I have vimentin positivity, one of the things that I could deduce is that I'm dealing with a sarcoma,

158
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:41,180
because I've got a malignant tumor, obviously I can't recognize it, the cells are poorly differentiated,

159
00:11:41,540 --> 00:11:42,720
and it's vimentin positive.

160
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:47,620
Playing along the same lines, let's say that the cells are desmond positive,

161
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:50,040
that would indicate that I'm dealing with muscle cells.

162
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:54,940
If they're GFAP positive, that would indicate that I'm dealing with neural glial cells.

163
00:11:55,320 --> 00:11:58,860
And if they're neurofilament positive, that would indicate that I'm dealing with neurons.

164
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:03,240
So these intermediate filaments are very high yield, because it goes back to histology,

165
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:09,920
and because we use these clinically, it's not uncommon for us to order a battery of immunohistochemical stains

166
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:11,960
when we're trying to subclassify tumor.

167
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:14,280
So you do need to be familiar with this.

168
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:18,900
Now, obviously I'm painting very broad strokes and highlighting some general principles.

169
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:24,500
There are some exceptions to these rules, but I think for your purposes, this is what you need to understand.

170
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,640
Another way that we could play the game is that, let's say we have a tumor,

171
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:33,340
and we know that it's a carcinoma, it's making glands,

172
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:37,640
and we're concerned about whether it might be from the prostate or it might be from another organ.

173
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:41,140
Well, we could do an immunohistochemical stain for PSA,

174
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:46,120
and if the cells are PSA positive, that would suggest that we're dealing with a prostate cancer.

175
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:48,420
Similarly with breast.

176
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:51,800
You know, if a woman has a metastatic cancer in the lung,

177
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:56,640
and I'm worried about where that tumor came from,

178
00:12:56,960 --> 00:13:02,700
one potential way of determining that would be to do an estrogen receptor stain,

179
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:05,580
and if the estrogen receptor is expressed in those cells,

180
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:08,940
it's possible that I'm dealing with a breast cancer,

181
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:13,020
because estrogen receptor is commonly seen in breast carcinoma.

182
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:17,380
Thyroglobulin would help me to identify a thyroid cancer.

183
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:23,080
Chromogranin would help me to identify neuroendocrine carcinomas.

184
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:30,020
And I would say from my personal experience that chromogranin is a relatively high-yield stain to be familiar with.

185
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:33,380
It's commonly tested by examiners.

186
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:39,740
The most well-differentiated and sort of best type of neuroendocrine cancer that you can get

187
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:41,020
is called a carcinoid tumor,

188
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:47,740
and the worst and most poorly differentiated neuroendocrine tumor that can develop is a small cell carcinoma.

189
00:13:48,260 --> 00:13:51,740
And so this is sort of a spectrum of tumors that arise from neuroendocrine cells.

190
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:56,320
It is important to understand that both small cell carcinoma and carcinoid tumors

191
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:59,580
are sort of derived from the same cell type, i.e. neuroendocrine cells,

192
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:04,860
and it is very high-yield to know that chromogranin is expressed in neuroendocrine cells.

193
00:14:05,340 --> 00:14:08,360
And then finally, the last of the stains I've listed here is S100,

194
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:13,460
and S100 is useful for many things, but it's particularly useful to mark melanoma.

195
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:18,060
So, you know, the way that examiners like to play the game with immunohistochemical stains

196
00:14:18,060 --> 00:14:21,880
is that they might say to you that there's a tumor and a biopsy is performed,

197
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:27,320
and the pathologist sends you a report saying that he sees or she sees keratin-positive cells.

198
00:14:27,700 --> 00:14:30,780
So they would then ask you, what type of tumor are you dealing with?

199
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:37,280
And the answers could, you know, the choices could be A, carcinoma, B, melanoma, C, lymphoma, D, sarcoma,

200
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:41,100
and obviously because it's keratin-positive, that would indicate epithelium,

201
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:44,060
which would then indicate that you have a carcinoma.

202
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:48,460
So I hope that I've made that clear because it is a relatively high-yield topic.

203
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:53,380
Here's a picture of an immunohistochemical stain just to let you know what one looks like.

204
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:57,760
Basically, this brown staining indicates that the cells are reacting

205
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:02,300
with the particular antibody that we've introduced in this test.

206
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:07,020
Now, another way by which we can classify tumors

207
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:13,880
and follow the treatment and response of tumors are serum tumor markers.

208
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:15,280
What are serum tumor markers?

209
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:18,340
These are proteins that are released by the tumor.

210
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:20,980
They are released, obviously, into the serum.

211
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:25,200
Now, these serum tumor markers can be useful in three circumstances.

212
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:29,580
Some serum tumor markers are useful as a screening test.

213
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:34,960
For example, PSA is a protein that's released by prostate cancer,

214
00:15:35,140 --> 00:15:38,300
and it gets released into the blood, and we can then detect it in the blood,

215
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:40,480
and so it's useful as a screening test.

216
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:43,920
That would be one use of a serum tumor marker.

217
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:48,160
Another use of serum tumor markers is to monitor response to treatment.

218
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:51,200
For example, patient has a prostate cancer.

219
00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:52,920
The PSA is 15.

220
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:59,540
The surgeon goes in and removes the prostate cancer and removes the prostate along with the cancer,

221
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:02,320
and after removing the prostate along with the cancer,

222
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:05,420
we would expect the PSA to drop to near zero.

223
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,380
And so this would be a way by which we would monitor response to treatment.

224
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:13,820
And then let's say that the patient comes back in, I don't know, two years later,

225
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:16,820
and when they come back in two years later, they have bone pain.

226
00:16:16,820 --> 00:16:23,860
Well, if we did a PSA and the PSA was now high, that would help to prove that perhaps the patient has a recurrence.

227
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:27,020
And so these are some of the uses for serum tumor markers.

228
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:30,460
I want to remind you that when it's used for screening,

229
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:36,180
elevated serum levels still require a tissue biopsy for diagnosis.

230
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,540
I think I've already hammered home this point.

231
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:42,600
You cannot make a diagnosis of cancer without a tissue biopsy.

232
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:48,900
And so even if a patient has a super high PSA, let's say, you know, some crazy number like 3-400,

233
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:55,060
that still would require a tissue biopsy before a diagnosis of carcinoma can be made.

234
00:16:55,440 --> 00:16:59,760
And so, for example, if I asked you a question where I said patient comes in for screening

235
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:04,880
and a PSA is performed and the PSA is markedly elevated, what's the next step?

236
00:17:05,540 --> 00:17:08,740
A surgery, B biopsy, CDE, etc.

237
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:17,560
The answer would be that you still need to biopsy the site and prove that it is tumor that is indeed causing that elevated PSA.

238
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:21,980
And that's because other things can elevate the PSA as well, not only cancer.

239
00:17:21,980 --> 00:17:29,200
Now, once a patient has a cancer and we've made the diagnosis of cancer,

240
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:34,660
the next thing that the pathologist does, which is important for prognosis, is to grade the cancer.

241
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:36,720
And what does grading of cancer involve?

242
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:43,300
It involves asking the question, does the tumor look like the tissue in which it's growing?

243
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:50,300
So the more the tumor looks like the parent tissue in which it's growing, the better differentiated it would be.

244
00:17:50,300 --> 00:17:55,680
And so, if it resembles the parent tissue, as I've stated here, it's well differentiated.

245
00:17:56,140 --> 00:18:00,580
And if it does not resemble the parent tissue, we call that poorly differentiated.

246
00:18:00,900 --> 00:18:02,620
How do we determine differentiation?

247
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:10,100
Well, we look at the architecture of the cells and ask, does the architecture resemble the normal architecture of that tissue?

248
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:15,320
And then we also look at the nuclear features, like, for example, the mitotic activity.

249
00:18:16,060 --> 00:18:20,160
Again, as I've mentioned, grading is important for determining prognosis.

250
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:26,180
The better differentiated a tumor is, or a cancer is, the better the prognosis.

251
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,180
So a well-differentiated cancer has a good prognosis.

252
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:33,080
A poorly differentiated cancer has a poor prognosis.

253
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:40,760
Another important feature of cancer that we need to be familiar with on a clinical level is staging.

254
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:46,120
And staging basically asks the question, how far has the cancer grown?

255
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:48,580
So what's its size or depth of invasion?

256
00:18:49,040 --> 00:18:51,160
And how far has the cancer spread?

257
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:55,100
So it's based on two things, size and spread of the cancer.

258
00:18:55,500 --> 00:18:58,380
Now, this is the key prognostic factor.

259
00:18:58,520 --> 00:19:01,140
It is the number one prognostic factor.

260
00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:03,020
It is more important than grade.

261
00:19:03,020 --> 00:19:07,500
And the final staging is really determined after resection of the tumor.

262
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:10,900
So clinically, we can stage a patient.

263
00:19:11,100 --> 00:19:17,000
We can try to determine what we think the clinical staging would be based on our clinical exam.

264
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:21,640
However, the true final staging is based on resection of the tumor.

265
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:30,420
When the tumor is removed and the pathologist analyzes the tissue, they then provide a final pathologic staging for the cancer.

266
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:32,860
Now, what system do we use for staging?

267
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:34,960
It's called the TNM system.

268
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:36,780
It looks at three key factors.

269
00:19:36,940 --> 00:19:41,880
It looks at the tumor and asks the question, how big is the tumor or how deep has the tumor invaded?

270
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:45,520
Generally speaking, tumor size is important in solid organs.

271
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:51,640
And generally speaking, tumor depth is important in organs that are tubular.

272
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:57,860
So, for example, the bowel, the depth of invasion is important in the bowel.

273
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:02,820
Whereas in the breast, size is important because it's a more solid organ.

274
00:20:03,740 --> 00:20:06,420
So that's the T part of the TNM staging.

275
00:20:06,940 --> 00:20:12,020
The N in the TNM refers to the spread to regional lymph nodes.

276
00:20:12,020 --> 00:20:14,780
This is the second most important prognostic factor.

277
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:20,140
And the M refers to distant metastasis, i.e. away from the regional lymph nodes.

278
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:24,560
And this is the single most important prognostic factor.

279
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:30,780
Metastasis is the single most important prognostic factor in the TNM staging system.

280
00:20:31,680 --> 00:20:34,340
And you do need to be aware of that as well.

281
00:20:34,860 --> 00:20:37,400
And so that really closes off our discussion of neoplasia.

282
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:40,980
Again, we've just laid out some very general principles.

283
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:44,420
Much of this is going to be repeated as we cover systems.

284
00:20:44,420 --> 00:20:47,160
But it's nice to have a little bit of a framework as we move forward.

285
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:48,980
And that was the goal of this lecture.

